===Etymology===
[[File:Ancient Greece Ostraca for Ostracism (27964951973).jpg|thumb|Three [[Ancient Greek]] [[ostracon|ostraca]] [[inscribe]]d with the [[name#Noun|names]] Themistokles and Neokleos,{{ref|group=n|name=n1|From the collection of the {{w|Goulandris Museum of Cycladic Art}} in [[Athens]], [[Greece]].}} which were used to [[vote#Verb|vote]] for whether those persons should be ostracized ''(sense 1)'' from the [[city]]; the [[procedure]] was called [[ostracism]].]]

From {{der|en|grc|ὀστρακίζω|t=to banish from a city by ostracism}}, from {{m|grc|ὄστρᾰκον|t=earthenware vessel; fragment of such a vessel, potsherd}} (from the fact that when voting was held to decide whether to banish people, their names were inscribed on potsherds) + {{m|grc|-ῐ́ζω|pos=''suffix forming {{glossary|verb}}s''}}). The English word is cognate with {{cog|fr|ostraciser}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=133175|date=September 2004|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

